=============================================================
  Joint Hypothesis Tests (Wald)
=============================================================

Error-correction term as a whole
  H0: alpha = 0
  Wald chi2(2) = 12.7413, p-value = 0.0017
  ! NOT A TEST: under H0, beta is unidentified (Davies).  Use -lrtest -bootstrap

Weak exogeneity, one variable at a time   [H0: row i of alpha = 0, chi2(1)]

muskrat (nabla Y2 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 6.7095, p-value = 0.0096
  REJECT H0 -> muskrat adjusts to the disequilibrium.

mink (Y1 block):
  H0: this variable does not adjust
  Wald chi2(1) = 0.6577, p-value = 0.4174
  Cannot reject H0 -> mink is weakly exogenous for B.

Exclusion from the cointegrating relations   [H0: row i of beta_2 = 0, chi2(1)]
  only the 1 nabla Y2 variable can be excluded: beta = [I_r ; beta_2] normalises on Y1

muskrat:
  H0: row 1 of beta_2 = 0
  Wald chi2(1) = 21.2336, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> muskrat belongs in the long-run relation.

Short-run dynamics   [Gamma on nabla Y, Theta on the innovations]
  ! statements about nabla Y, not about the levels: a variable can drive
    another's DIFFERENCES and still be tied to it only through ec

Joint significance of the last short-run lag, Gamma(1):
  H0: Gamma(last) = 0
  Wald chi2(4) = 28.5989, p-value = 0.0000
  REJECT H0 -> the last AR lag is significant.

All cross effects jointly:
  H0: every off-diagonal coefficient of Gamma and Theta = 0
  Wald chi2(2) = 15.1399, p-value = 0.0005
  REJECT H0 -> the cross structure earns its parameters.

D.muskrat: what the others do to it:
  H0: no other variable enters the equation of D.muskrat
  Wald chi2(1) = 8.0257, p-value = 0.0046
  REJECT H0 -> D.muskrat is driven by the others.

D.muskrat: what it does to the others:
  H0: D.muskrat enters no other equation
  Wald chi2(1) = 8.2091, p-value = 0.0042
  REJECT H0 -> D.muskrat drives the others.

D.mink: what the others do to it:
  H0: no other variable enters the equation of D.mink
  Wald chi2(1) = 8.2091, p-value = 0.0042
  REJECT H0 -> D.mink is driven by the others.

D.mink: what it does to the others:
  H0: D.mink enters no other equation
  Wald chi2(1) = 8.0257, p-value = 0.0046
  REJECT H0 -> D.mink drives the others.

  ! p-values from the BFGS-accumulated covariance; use -fdhess before quoting them


=============================================================
  Impulse Response of the Levels
=============================================================

  Response of Y_{t+k} to a one-s.d. orthogonalized shock at t.
  Shocks are orthogonalized by Sigma = P D P', so the ORDERING is the
  .inp's column order: a different order gives different shocks.
  ! in a cointegrated system the response does not die out: what it
    converges to is the permanent effect, of rank M - r = 1.

